  "sourceNotes": {
    "raw": "The choice question 定 (ding6) connects two options, meaning 'or'. In Cantonese, it appears between the choices: 咖啡定奶茶 literally means 'coffee or tea'. Unlike English 'or', 定 cannot be used for negative choices or statements - only for offering alternatives in questions.\n\nThe comparative structure uses 好啲 (hou2 di1) or [adjective]啲 to mean 'better' or 'more [adjective]'. The character 啲 functions as a comparative marker here, different from its use as a plural classifier. Note 鍾意...多啲 (zung1 yi3...do1 di1) for preferences: 'like...more'. The structure splits: verb + object + 多啲 creates 'like X more'.\n\nThe phrase 你覺得呢 (nei5 gok3 dak1 ne1) asks for opinions, literally 'you feel what'. The particle 呢 (ne1) at the end softens the question and invites response. 都係 (dou1 hai6) means 'also is' or 'also' for agreement - it shows you share the same choice or opinion.\n\nPronunciation watch: 定 (ding6) has a low falling tone, not to be confused with the high level tone. The word 香 (hoeng1) means 'fragrant' for describing pleasant food and drink aromas - very common in Cantonese food culture. Common mistake: saying 好過 (hou2 gwo3) instead of 好啲 in this context - 好過 is more emphatic and suggests stronger preference."
  }
